itten Assessment - Planning.md#1 omits '*obtinere*' (to occupy). #2 and #3 include it in the translation, although #2 more accurately translates the whole clause, including 'as has been said' as a translation of 'dictum' (a perfect participle in the passive). #3 states that Gallic territory 'is bounded by' the Garonne, however, '*initium*' translates directly as 'starts' or 'begins' as in #1 and #2. This error may have been created by a translation of the next clause, '*continetur Garumna flumine*' whilst ignoring '*initium*'. Another variance in translation as above between 'territory', 'borders', and 'country' - all are accurate. #2 omits '*etiam*'. #1 translates 'attingit' as reaches, but the latter translations use 'touches', which seems to be the better translation in context. #3 brings the final clause, '*vergit ad septentriones*' to the beginning of the passage, which allows one to connect it with the 'region occupied by the Celts', however there is no evidence to support the translation that the region 'has' a frontier facing north; likewise, 'its general trend' is an inaccurate translation of '*vergit*', which should be translated as '*faces*' as in the former translation. #3 also includes 'inhabited' which is not present in the Latin.","lines":[121,128],"size":1768,"outlinks":[],"class_name":"SmartBlock"},
"smart_blocks:Latin - Semester 1/Latin Written Assessment - Planning.md#1 omits '*obtinere*' (to occupy). #2 and #3 include it in the translation, although #2 more accurately translates the whole clause, including 'as has been said' as a translation of 'dictum' (a perfect participle in the passive). #3 states that Gallic territory 'is bounded by' the Garonne, however, '*initium*' translates directly as 'starts' or 'begins' as in #1 and #2. This error may have been created by a translation of the next clause, '*continetur Garumna flumine*' whilst ignoring '*initium*'. Another variance in translation as above between 'territory', 'borders', and 'country' - all are accurate. #2 omits '*etiam*'. #1 translates 'attingit' as reaches, but the latter translations use 'touches', which seems to be the better translation in context. #3 brings the final clause, '*vergit ad septentriones*' to the beginning of the passage, which allows one to connect it with the 'region occupied by the Celts', however there is no evidence to support the translation that the region 'has' a frontier facing north; likewise, 'its general trend' is an inaccurate translation of '*vergit*', which should be translated as '*faces*' as in the former translation. #3 also includes 'inhabited' which is not present in the Latin.#{1}": {"path":null,"embeddings":{"TaylorAI/bge-micro-v2":{"vec":[-0.01034481,-0.04072833,0.05435119,-0.00045645,-0.00927395,-0.06113592,-0.02952103,0.04268984,0.01700998,-0.09037916,-0.03229032,-0.13868143,-0.0381395,0.00267962,0.01792632,-0.05581579,-0.04988234,0.03177674,0.01669801,0.01103921,0.07822769,-0.04852774,0.04431076,0.00382408,0.09577541,0.09030797,0.03406868,-0.01716548,-0.0323264,-0.24182498,-0.01830577,0.00642643,-0.04207394,-0.02059702,-0.04776815,0.02665665,-0.06384328,-0.0071263,-0.05118781,0.0018363,0.05335241,0.06264421,-0.01862684,-0.06791839,-0.00994009,-0.00527226,-0.04008382,0.04021014,-0.06734811,-0.03493668,-0.0008547,0.02324494,0.02004591,0.0194613,0.02141133